events.text:
## Plan (day 1, before painting)
Canvas: 700 mm, aspect 1.35 (W1000 x H741), linen 15x13, seed 1822. Ground: knife layer red earth 2/ochre 2/white 1 (60um) under brushed lead white 10/ochre 0.7/umber 0.25 (35um) -> pale cream with brush striations.

Subject (my own): "Oak on the ridge at evening". Axis x=500. Bands parallel to the picture plane:
- dark foreground ridge, a low hump rising to the axis (top ~y590 at centre, ~640 at edges), bare;
- on its crest, on the axis, one old retrenched oak, bare, silhouetted, drawn exactly (stag-head dead limbs at top, uneven crown masses), ~440 units tall;
- no middle ground: the land drops away to a valley full of mist (pale band);
- far low mountain line, cool violet-grey, top ~y505-520, foot lost in mist ~560;
- sky ~70% of height, after sunset: brightest band at horizon behind the tree (pale yellow / peach), through pale greenish neutral, to deep smalt-cobalt blue at top. Perhaps one thin long cloud band across the glow. Maybe evening star, small, off axis.
Kinds of things: ridge, oak, mist/mountains, sky (+star). No figure.
Method: pencil underdrawing; sky first in thin graded body paint blended wet, then a refining layer; land; tree last over dry sky with riggers/rounds.

